rubrique.texte:
UIC and EURNEX are delighted to invite you to their upcoming webinar on the opportunities and challenges of Artificial Intelligence. The session will be held on 22 January 2026, 9:00 to 12:00 CET (16:00 to 19:00 CST China). 

{{Motivation and background}}

The global railway sector is currently undergoing widespread transformation, driven by digitalisation, artificial intelligence, and advanced data analytics. Both China and Europe are pioneers in rail technology, yet they often approach innovation from different perspectives shaped by their unique operational, cultural, and regulatory environments. 

This workshop aims to create a platform for dialogue, knowledge sharing, and scientific collaboration between Chinese and European stakeholders. The event aims to foster mutual understanding, identify shared challenges, and explore opportunities for joint innovation in the scientific community that can shape the future of sustainable and intelligent rail mobility.

{{Organising committee}}

The European organising committee comprises {{EURNEX and UIC}}, with support from {{Beijing Jiaotong University (BJTU)}} in China. It brings together representatives from leading universities, railway undertakings, infrastructure managers, and research institutes across Europe and China, ensuring that the discussions reflect a wide spectrum of expertise and perspectives.

{{Agenda}}

The workshop will present the status of AI in the rail sector in both China and Europe. Next, the opportunities and challenges will be shared during the panel discussion. 

The speakers will focus on different fields of expertise:

09:00 – Welcome address
-* Representatives from Eurnex, Beijing Jiaotong University (BJTU), and UIC

09:10 – Speakers China
-* 15 min – Prof Liping Jing,  BJTU – {Exploring AI in railways}
-* 15 min – Dr Ping Li, China Academy of Railway Sciences – {The innovation and development of intelligent high-speed railway in China}
-* 15 min – Prof Guoqing Jing,  BJTU – {AI and automation for railway engineering in China}
-* 15 min – Q&A session

10:15 – Speakers Europe
-* Dr Francesco Flammini, University of Applied Sciences and Arts of Southern Switzerland (SUPSI) – {From automation to autonomy in intelligent train control and smart-railways}
-* Dr Kenza Harkouken Saiah, Alstom – {Enhancing railway efficiency, sustainability, and reliability through trustworthy AI for predictive maintenance and energy optimisation}
-* Johan Jonsson, Trafikverket – {Topic to be confirmed}
-* Q&A session

11:20 –  Panel discussion
-* Featuring all previous speakers and moderated by UIC

11:50-12:00 – Wrap-up and conclusion
-* By Eurnex/UIC

{{Register here: https://lnkd.in/d6Uevfke}}

The UIC Passenger Department is proud to announce the inaugural edition of its {{Passenger Week}}, taking place onsite from {{13 to 16 April 2026}} at the {{UIC Headquarters in Paris}}.

This landmark event will bring together the {{global railway passenger community}}, including {{CEOs and top executives from railway passenger business units}}, as well as {{leading mobility stakeholders and partners}}, to share knowledge, explore innovation, and shape the future of passenger mobility services.

Designed as a high-level platform for collaboration and strategic dialogue, Passenger Week will feature a rich programme of {{expert sessions, open discussions, and executive-level meetings}}, fostering alignment between strategy, operations, and innovation across passenger transport.

{{Programme highlights}}

-* {{Monday 13 April – Expert working group sessions}}
The week will open with a day dedicated to UIC working group experts. These sessions will provide a shared environment for technical discussions and ongoing project development within the UIC Passenger groups.

-* {{Tuesday 14 April – Open Day: Multimodality and innovation in focus}}
The Open Day, accessible to all stakeholders and interested participants, will be the highlight of the week.
-**	{{Morning session}}: A look into {{multimodal ticketing}}, exploring current challenges and future opportunities in seamless travel.
-**	{{Afternoon conference}}: A high-level gathering of key industry stakeholders to discuss the {{sector’s needs}} and the {{UIC-developed tools}} to support these.
Four dedicated {{solution panels}} will address:
-**	{{Standards}}
-**	{{User experience & multimodality}}
-**	{{Sustainability & socio-economic impact}}
-**	{{Innovation}}

-*  {{Wednesday 15 and Thursday 16 April  – Passenger Services Group & Global Passenger Forum}}
The final two days will host the {{Passenger Services Group (PSG)}} and the {{Global Passenger Forum (GPF)}}, which are key governance and strategic meetings for UIC passenger activities.

{{Join us in Paris to connect, work together, and contribute to the future of passenger rail!}}

-* {{Dates}}: 13–16 April 2026
-* {{Location}}: UIC Headquarters, Paris
-* {{Registration}}: https://evenium.events/uicpassengerweek/
-* {{More about the UIC Passenger Department}}: https://uic.org/passenger/

Together for global excellence – shaping passenger mobility services

UIC is delighted to be launching its call for contributions to its upcoming Workshop on Cleaning Protocols for Railway Service, an online event taking place on 16 January 2026 (09:00-12:30 CET). The workshop will bring together international experts to review current cleaning practices and explore innovative approaches covering onboard services, stations, and rolling stock maintenance, with a strong focus on operational efficiency, service quality, and passenger satisfaction.

The programme aims to showcase real-world practices and case studies, address operational challenges, and foster discussions between railway stakeholders with the objective of harmonising cleaning standards and procedures across the sector.

{{Who should apply}}

UIC welcomes proposals from a wide range of stakeholders involved in railway cleaning and service quality. This includes railway undertakings and infrastructure managers seeking to share operational experience, cleaning service providers with hands-on expertise, research institutions contributing analytical or scientific perspectives, and industry partners developing products, systems, or solutions relevant to cleaning activities.

{{Potential topics}}

The contributions may address, but are not limited to, practical and strategic aspects of cleaning operations. Relevant themes include onboard cleaning procedures and turnaround operations, station cleaning strategies with particular attention to the management of high-traffic areas, and deep-cleaning processes for rolling stock carried out in depots. Submissions may also focus on quality assurance approaches, auditing methods, and performance monitoring, as well as innovation such as digital tools and emerging technologies applied to cleaning. Topics related to training, workforce management, and safety considerations are also strongly encouraged.

{{How to contribute}}

Speakers are invited to submit a proposal including a title, a 200 to 300-word abstract, and a short biography of up to 150 words. The submission deadline is Tuesday, 23 December 2025. Presentations are expected to last 10-20 minutes followed by Q&A, and the working language of the workshop will be English. Proposals should be sent by email to perez@uic.org. The selected speakers will be contacted in early January.

Participants and prospective speakers can also register directly via the event registration page on the UIC website: [UIC - International union of railways->https://uic.org/events/workshop-on-cleaning-protocols-for-railway-service]

